>
<h1>What Do You Need Your Conveyor to Do?</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Stop shopping by equipment name. Start with the job your line needs done. Every function below links to the conveyor types and configurations that solve that specific problem.</p>
<p><strong>Conveyor functions</strong> describe the specific tasks a conveyor performs inside a packaging line, accumulating, indexing, rejecting, laning, loading trays, sorting, merging, or elevating product. Selecting a conveyor by function rather than by equipment type ensures the system matches the actual production requirement. Most packaging lines combine several distinct conveyor functions between primary packaging and palletizing.</p>
<h2 id="functions-heading">Browse by Function</h2>
<p>Each card represents a distinct packaging line task and links to a guide covering how it works, technology options, and selection criteria.</p>
<ul class="usa-collection">{functions.map((fn) => (
<li class="usa-collection__item"><div class="usa-collection__body"><h3 class="usa-collection__heading"><a class="usa-link" href={fn.slug.startsWith('/') ? `${fn.slug}/` : `/conveyor-functions/${fn.slug}/`}>{fn.name}</a></h3><p class="usa-collection__description">{fn.desc}</p></div></li>
))}</ul>
<h2 id="howto-heading">Function-First Selection</h2>
<p>Most packaging engineers start conveyor selection by asking "What brand of belt conveyor should I buy?" That approach skips the most important step: defining what the conveyor must <em>do</em>. Two plants running the same product at the same speed can need completely different conveyor configurations if their line layouts, accumulation requirements, or reject rates differ.</p>
<p>The function-first approach works in three steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Map every transition</strong> between machines on your packaging line. Each transition is a conveyor function, accumulate, index, lane, reject, merge, or elevate.</li>
<li><strong>Define requirements per function</strong>: throughput, product size, weight, orientation, accumulation time, environment (washdown, cleanroom), and guarding. <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a> is the US safety standard for conveyor design, installation, and operation.</li>
<li><strong>Select conveyor type per function</strong>, a single line may use <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a> for case transport, <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> for accumulation, and a dedicated <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> after the checkweigher.</li>
</ol>
<p>Define the signals at each transition too. Machines that follow <a href="https://www.omac.org/packml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PackML</a>, the OMAC machine-state standard adopted by ISA as TR88.00.02, report states such as running, held, and stopped in a common way, which makes it easier to program each conveyor to accumulate, meter, or stop in response. <a href="https://www.pmmi.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PMMI</a>, the Association for Packaging and Processing Technologies, represents the machine builders who supply many of these functions.</p>
<p>This approach prevents the common mistake of over-specifying expensive conveyor types where simple gravity sections would suffice, or under-specifying critical accumulation and reject zones that cause line stoppages. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for how function selection affects project budgets.</p>
