<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a conveyor function?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor function describes the specific task a conveyor performs, accumulating, indexing, rejecting, laning, or loading trays. Packaging lines typically require several distinct functions between the primary packaging machine and the palletizer. Selecting by function ensures you match equipment to the actual production need rather than defaulting to a familiar conveyor brand or type.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How do I choose the right conveyor function for my line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Map every machine-to-machine transition on your packaging line. Each transition represents a conveyor function. Common sequences include: filler to capper (indexing), capper to labeler (accumulation), labeler to checkweigher (transport), checkweigher to case packer (<a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject</a> + <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning</a>), and case packer to palletizer (accumulation + merge).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Can one conveyor perform multiple functions?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes, some conveyors combine functions. An <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR accumulation conveyor</a> can buffer product and meter release (combining accumulation with basic indexing). A <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning conveyor</a> often includes upstream accumulation zones. However, combining too many functions into a single section usually compromises performance. Dedicated function-specific conveyors outperform multi-function compromises in most high-throughput applications.</p>

</div>
</div>
